@dottodot Looks like IE's concept of "session cookies" is cookies with a zero/past ttl:

> From https://www.microsoft.com/resources/documentation/windows/xp/all/proddocs/en-us/set_priv_custom.mspx?mfr=true
> 
> If you want Internet Explorer to always allow session cookies (cookies that will be deleted from your computer when you close Internet Explorer) to be saved on your computer, click Always allow session cookies.

So in other words, IE doesn't know that `sails.sid` happens to relate to user sessions in your app-- and I don't believe that's what they mean (I think they'd call `sails.sid` an ["authentication cookie"](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_cookie)).  When they say "session cookie" they mean something different: like a "transient cookie".  That is, if a user's IE settings are configured to only allow 1st party cookies if they are "session cookies", it means that IE will only allow cookies that self-destruct when the user closes their web browser.

---

**tldr;** you need to make cookies transient for IE users with this particular use case.  To do so, you _could_ set a past expiry date for your session cookie (e.g. something like [this vanilla express example](http://stackoverflow.com/a/4371696/486547)).  Or you could do so globally in your app by changing your `sails.config.session` settings.

But what I'd recommend is this (on a per-request basis):

```
// If you detect that this is a request from a user agent with this requirement, then set:
req.session.cookie.expires = false;
```

> From https://github.com/expressjs/session#reqsessioncookie:
> 
> Each session has a unique cookie object accompany it. This allows you to alter the session cookie per visitor. For example we can set req.session.cookie.expires to false to enable the cookie to remain for only the duration of the user-agent.

I believe you will want to do this on _every request_ from the user agent (potentially even for assets), so it's the kind of thing you'll probably want to put in a hook.

For example:

``` javascript
// api/hooks/transient-sessions/index.js
module.exports = function (sails) {
  return {

    initialize: function (done){
      // Only relevant if the session hook is in use (refuse to continue lifting otherwise)
      if (!sails.hooks.session) {
        return done(new Error('In order for this `transient-sessions` hook to be meaningful, the `session` hook must be enabled.'));
      }
      return done();
    },

    routes: {
      before: {
        '/*': function (req, res, next){
          var isUserAgentSuspiciousOfTheWholeWorld;
          // ...e.g. maybe determine this by looking at the user-agent header ...

          if (isUserAgentSuspiciousOfTheWholeWorld) {
            req.session.cookie.expires = false;
          }
          return next();
        }//</*>
      }
    }

  };
};
```

Ok so I have the following which works great, not sure if it the best way of doing it but as it works I'm happy.

Basically your example was fine but meant that users of IE that had normal settings would lose their session once they closed the browser so would lose things such as their cart so I found that by trying to set a cookie and checking to see if it exists allows me to target users we these silly settings.

```
var parser = require('ua-parser-js');

module.exports = function(sails) {
  return {

    initialize: function(done) {
      // Only relevant if the session hook is in use (refuse to continue lifting otherwise)
      if (!sails.hooks.session) {
        return done(new Error('In order for this `transient-sessions` hook to be meaningful, the `session` hook must be enabled.'));
      }
      return done();
    },

    routes: {
      before: {
        '/*': function(req, res, next) {
          res.cookie('tryset',true, { maxAge: 900000, httpOnly: true });
          var ua = parser(req.headers['user-agent']);
          var isUserAgentSuspiciousOfTheWholeWorld;
          if (ua.browser.name === 'IE' && !req.cookies.tryset) {
            isUserAgentSuspiciousOfTheWholeWorld = true;
          } else if (ua.browser.name === 'IE' && req.cookies.tryset) {
            req.session.cookie.expires = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000 * 30;
          }

          if (isUserAgentSuspiciousOfTheWholeWorld) {
            req.session.cookie.expires = false;
          }
          return next();
        }
      }
    }
  };
};
```
